Decide a phone's viewport from where the tab actually is.

Two bugs with one cause. Web preferences are computed once per SiteInstance,
and after a navigation content only calls OverrideWebPreferencesAfterNavigation
-- so anything decided from the current URL is decided once, when the tab's
renderer is created, and never revisited.

chrome:// pages therefore stayed on the mobile rules that 8dc604d was supposed
to lift them out of. A tab that starts on a website and then opens
chrome://version was still laid out at 980px and shrunk to fit: 980 into about
345vp of screen is roughly a third, which is why its text measured about 5vp.

Asking for the desktop site had the same shape of problem from the other side.
content turns the page's <meta viewport> off when the navigation entry
overrides the user agent, a few lines before it calls the embedder -- and then
this code ran and turned it back on. Swapping only the user agent leaves a
responsive site reading the phone's width and laying out for a phone, which is
exactly what it is designed to do.

The rules now live in one function that both hooks call, and it reports
whether it changed anything so the after-navigation hook can send the result.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

